.TH GXWI-DISK-MANAGER 1 "2026-10-06" "Peios"
.SH NAME
gxwi-disk-manager \- see this machine's disks, partitions and mounts, on a GXWI desktop
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B gxwi-disk-manager
.SH DESCRIPTION
Disk Manager is the desktop's window on this machine's storage. Down the
side are the disks, then Mounts and At Startup. A disk's page gives its
model, size, device and partition table, and a bar that draws the disk from
start to end: each partition as wide as its share, in the colour of what it
holds, with free space striped. The list under the bar says the same in
words. Pressing a partition opens it: its filesystem, label, UUID and how
full it is; where it is mounted; and its number, type, name and place on the
disk.
.PP
Every mount has a mount policy: what Peios does with a file that has no
permissions of its own. Disk Manager names them by what they do: Deny
Access, Give Permissions, Not Saved, Give Permissions and Save Them, and Not
Managed. Permissions... shows, in the permissions editor, the template that
files are given where their folder has none to pass on. Mounts lists
everything mounted, with its options and policy, and for a StrataFS view its
strata. At Startup lists what is mounted every time the machine starts.
.PP
The forms are all here: Erase Disk, adding and deleting a partition, Format
(ext4 or FAT32, and for ext4 the permissions of its top folder), Mount,
Unmount, a mount's policy, At Startup and Eject. None of them changes
anything yet. Changes are made by a privileged disk service, which is not
built; until it is, each change is checked and asked about as it will be,
then ends by saying on the status line that nothing was changed. Until then,
use part, mke2fs and mount.
.PP
Changing disks needs the Manage Volumes privilege, which Administrators
hold. Anyone else sees everything there is to see, with the reason said
once, and nothing to press. Opening a disk to read its partition table and
filesystems is, as shipped, for SYSTEM and Administrators only; for anyone
else a disk's partitions are still listed, a mounted filesystem's type comes
from the mount table, and the rest is marked Not Readable or Contents
unknown. A mount's policy can only be read with Manage Volumes too.
.PP
It reads as the person, and changes nothing: lsblk for the disks, sysfs for
where partitions lie, the kernel's mount table, statvfs for how full each
filesystem is, the kernel for each mount's policy, and stratafs for the
system's views. It looks every two seconds, so a disk plugged in or taken
out, or anything mounted or unmounted elsewhere, shows within a couple of
seconds.
.PP
It takes no arguments. Without a desktop to open on, it says so and ends.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/apps/dev.peios.gxwi-disk-manager.toml
Its application declaration, listed in a launcher as Disk Manager.
.TP
.I /proc/self/mountinfo
The kernel's mount table, which it reads for what is mounted.
.TP
.I /sys/class/block
Where it reads each partition's place on its disk.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lsblk (8),
.BR gxwi-sd-editor (1).
.PP
Disk Manager, at learn.peios.org.
